<!--
The single canonical entry for the beet website, and the one place its cloud
resources are declared. Discovery loads it locally when the `beet` binary runs
from the `site/` directory (`cd site && beet serve --server=http`), and the
deployed Lightsail box loads the same entry directly from the app bucket root.
The workspace commands that take an entry as an argument (`beet check site`,
`beet export-static site`) load it from the repo root.

The root is a one-shot `<CliServer always=true {CallOnReady}>` dispatcher whose
`Router` is its child, so the entry is a set of commands and the served site is ONE
of them: `serve`. `always=true` makes the dispatcher run on every boot regardless of
`--server`, since `--server` selects the *served site's* transports, not this
entry's. The box launches `app --repo=s3://.. --server=http,ssh serve` and a
developer launches `beet --main=site serve --server=http`: one process, one
argv, the same verb, so local and deployed take the same path.

    beet --main=site serve --server=http      # the website over http
    beet --main=site serve --server=tui       # ..as a live terminal (like `beet present`)
    beet --main=site serve --server=http,ssh  # ..http plus a multi-tenant ssh terminal

A `Router` is a url space, so the site's own routes stay rooted at `/` despite
hanging off the `serve` command, and no http/ssh request can reach a sibling
command route.

## One declaration, two meanings

`<DynamoTableBlock bx:ref="analytics" label="analytics"/>` is declared once, under
the stage `<Stack>`. The DEPLOY provisions it into that stack, and the RUNTIME
attaches a store for it, which the content router names with `StoreRef($analytics)`
(a `bx:ref` is document-global, and the declaration builds in every binary, so the
serving side reaches it from a different branch of the tree). Both sides compose
`<app>--<stage>--analytics` through the one resolved `Stack`, whose app name comes
from this entry's `<PackageConfig/>`, so they cannot disagree. The live site once
spent a whole deploy writing to a table that did not exist because the two sides
derived that name independently.

A resource belongs to the stack it is authored under and to no other. There is no
adoption: a declaration outside every `<Stack>` still resolves its runtime name,
but no deploy provisions it, which is a structural answer to "which stack owns
this?" rather than one inferred from the process stage.

## Analytics, and what outlives it

Raw per-event detail stops being useful after weeks; the aggregate trend is what
the site records analytics FOR and must never be lost. Retention is therefore
three things and **the order is the point**: a day's raw events are archived cold
into the archive bucket (`analytics/raw/<date>.ndjson.gz`), rolled up into
daily `analytics-rollup` aggregates that persist forever, and only then given the
`ttl` DynamoDB expires them by. No row is ever stamped before both the archive
object and the aggregate are confirmed present, so deletion from the table is a
cost decision rather than information loss — the whole history is a few megabytes
compressed.

`<Route path="jobs">` hosts that job and the deployed `rollup` lambda is the only
thing that boots it: the schedule invokes the function, the function's `bootstrap`
loads THIS document out of the app bucket and dispatches `rollup` into the `jobs`
router. The function publishes no url, no gateway and no hostname, so a sweep
that rewrites every row in the table is reachable by nothing but its timer, and
the site's own router is a different url space entirely.

    beet --main=site jobs                        # the job host, over http
    beet analytics summary                       # aggregates + the recent raws
    beet analytics summary --remote --bucket beet-site--prod--analytics

## The served site

`<Route path="serve" {(HttpServer, TuiServer, SshTuiServer)}>` is the serve route
and the server host in one: dispatching it calls the route's `RunningSet`, which
parks and starts the three long-running servers (`HttpServer`, the local live
`TuiServer`, and the multi-tenant `SshTuiServer`). They dispatch into the content
`Router` child, whose middleware and layout attach as component spreads: request
logging, analytics, `--help`, terminal link navigation, the layout, and the cache
policy (`CacheHeadersMiddleware` + `CacheHeaders` mark html pages edge-cacheable,
which the Cloudflare proxy honors and the deploy/sync purge refreshes).
`<DefaultAppRoutes/>` adds the default app routes (`/app-info`, the reactivity
runtime, …), `<RoutesDir/>` discovers the `routes/` pages, and `<AssetsDir
src="assets" cache="1h"/>` mounts the assets the site and blog reference,
browser-cacheable for an hour.

`site/assets` is a real directory whose source of record is the site's own shared
bucket (`beet-site--shared--assets`), hydrated with `just site-shared pull`. A
few files it serves are BUILT in the workspace tree (the wasm binary the tutorial
embeds, the geoip database, the perceive-act robot faces); `<DirCopy/>` is the
one declaration of what is borrowed, and runs ahead of every publish.

## The deploy

Structure is universal, so this same file loads whole in the deployed binary
(built `--no-default-features --features aws_sdk,ssh,geoip`): the deploy tags it
never linked build as inert entities, quietly, because the stack declares
`RequireFeatures(["infra","extra"])`. That declaration is also the loudness:
dispatching any deploy verb in a binary without those features fails naming
them and the rebuild that fixes it, before any step runs. That is what lets the
application declare its own infrastructure without shipping the deployer.

`<Stack>` is the identity every resource beneath it composes its name from, and it
is a registered component in every native build rather than a template prop, so the
lean binary reads the same stage and the same `shared` override off the same tree.
`<DeployRoutes/>` (a beet_extra template, absent from the lean binary along with the
verbs it hosts) adds the tofu lifecycle routes `validate`/`plan`/`apply`/`show`/
`list`/`destroy`/`rollback`/`rollforward` to whichever stack hosts it.

The deploy route applies once per layer, and the order is the point: `<TofuApply
layer="storage"/>` brings up the stores (the app bucket, the analytics table),
`<DirSync/>` fills them, and a bare `<TofuApply/>` converges the whole stack:
it uploads the built binary under the deploy id, publishes the release pointer
naming it, and converges the instance against a bucket that already holds the
site. One apply boots the new instance first, so it finds an empty bucket and no
entry document. Naming a layer no block declares is a loud error, never a silent
skip.

Most deploys do NOT replace the box. The instance's cloud-init renders machine
config only (Caddy, the sshd relocation, the CloudWatch agent, the unit), never a
version, so a code-only deploy plans no change to it; `<LightsailRelease/>` then
reaches the box over the management sshd on 2222 and rolls the unit onto the new
binary, verifying the RUNNING process reports this deploy's id. A machine-config
change still replaces the box, and rotates its access key with it. Either way the
static IP re-attaches so DNS never moves, and the ssh host key rides the env
channel so the fingerprint never changes.

Cloudflare fronts the box: http hostnames are proxied and edge-cached `A` records
at the static IP, ssh (and future live apps) ride the DNS-only `app.beet.org` /
`app.dev.beet.org` hostnames straight to it, `<CloudflareZoneSetup/>` converges
the zone-level edge config, and every deploy or sync ends with a
`<CloudflarePurgeCache/>`. On the deploy route the purge comes *after*
`<LightsailWatch/>`, since purging while the old instance still serves just
re-caches the old responses for the full edge TTL.

The assets bucket lives on its own `shared` stage under the `shared/` route
prefix, provisioned as its own step since a source shared by developers is a
different process from a stage deploy. No stage deploy or destroy touches it.

    just beet-deploy [--stage=prod]        # build -> stores -> sync -> release -> watch -> purge
    just beet-sync                         # re-publish the site (no redeploy)
    just beet-watch                        # tail the instance logs
    just site-shared apply                 # provision the site assets bucket
    just site-shared pull                  # hydrate site/assets from it (no credentials)
    just site-shared push                  # publish site/assets to it

Live deploy needs AWS credentials + `BEET_SSH_HOST_KEY`/`CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID`/
`CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` in `.env`, plus `tofu` and `cargo-zigbuild`.
-->
<CliServer always=true {CallOnReady}>
<Router {HelpHandler}>
	<!-- the ONE location app identity lives: the deploy names its resources from
	     this, and so does the running binary. -->
	<PackageConfig
		title="Beet"
		app_name="beet-site"
		description="A malleable engine for sovereign software"
		version="0.0.9-dev.16"
		homepage="https://beet.org"
		social_image="https://beet.org/assets/branding/social-card.png"
		theme_color="#006c4f"/>
	<Route path="serve" {(HttpServer, TuiServer, SshTuiServer)}>
		<Router {(RequestLogger, AnalyticsMiddleware, AnalyticsConfig, GeoIpDb, StoreRef($analytics), CacheHeadersMiddleware, CacheHeaders, HelpHandler, NavigateHandler, BsxLayout{template:"Layout"})}>
			<!-- the brand palette, one key colour per Material palette. The neutral
			     is split per mode: light is the handout cream (print), dark is the
			     green-cast ink (terminal phosphor). See docs/design/beet-branding. -->
			<Theme
				color="#006c4f"
				primary="#006c4f"
				secondary="#f028a8"
				tertiary="#ea8a0c"
				neutral_light="#f4eede"
				neutral_dark="#14211d"
				neutral_variant="#6a7772"
				error="#de3730"/>
			<TemplateDir src="templates"/>
			<Styles/>
			<DefaultAppRoutes/>
			<RoutesDir src="routes"/>
			<!-- the urls the blog served at before its posts were named: the files
			     stay numbered so they read in order, and every link ever published
			     keeps resolving, with a 301 to the name. -->
			<Route path="blog">
				<Redirect path="post-1" redirect="full-stack-bevy"/>
				<Redirect path="post-2" redirect="ecs-router"/>
				<Redirect path="post-3" redirect="bevys-five-and-beets-alive"/>
				<Redirect path="post-4" redirect="action-time"/>
				<Redirect path="post-5" redirect="declarative-state"/>
				<Redirect path="post-6" redirect="folk-technology"/>
				<Redirect path="post-7" redirect="malleable-application-framework"/>
				<Redirect path="post-8" redirect="its-all-been-done-before"/>
				<Redirect path="post-9" redirect="multi-interface-applications"/>
				<Redirect path="post-10" redirect="application-level-homoiconicity"/>
				<Redirect path="post-11" redirect="user-modifiable-users"/>
				<Redirect path="post-12" redirect="gentle-slopes-up-lonely-mountains"/>
				<Redirect path="post-13" redirect="engines-not-frameworks"/>
				<Redirect path="post-14" redirect="atproto-isnt-malleable-yet"/>
				<Redirect path="post-15" redirect="bevy-standardizes-malleable-software"/>
			</Route>
			<AssetsDir src="assets" cache="1h"/>
		</Router>
	</Route>
	<!-- the nightly job host: its own url space and its own process. The rollup
	     lambda is the only thing that ever boots this verb, so a table sweep is
	     unreachable from the served site even though both live in this file. -->
	<Route path="jobs" {HttpServer}>
		<Router>
			<Route path="rollup" {(AnalyticsRollupJob, StoreRef($analytics), RollupStoreRef($rollup), ArchiveStoreRef($archive))}/>
		</Router>
	</Route>
	<!-- the stage stack: every resource the running site needs, the verbs that
	     provision them, and the one `RequireFeatures` guarding the whole subtree -->
	<Stack {RequireFeatures(["infra","extra"])}>
		<!-- declared once: the deploy creates it, the runtime records to it. `ttl`
		     is the attribute the nightly job stamps, and it stamps nothing until
		     that day's cold archive and daily aggregate both exist. -->
		<DynamoTableBlock bx:ref="analytics" label="analytics" ttl="ttl"/>
		<!-- what the raws reduce to. Its own table because mixing row shapes would
		     make every event scan warn-skip the aggregates, and because expiry is
		     a property of the events table alone: these rows are forever. -->
		<DynamoTableBlock bx:ref="rollup" label="analytics-rollup"/>
		<S3BucketBlock label="app" deploy_versioned=false/>
		<!-- the `archive` profile: append-only cold data, the inverse of the app
		     bucket by lifecycle and by grant. Never touched by a deploy sync
		     (which would prune it), and the only bucket the running process may
		     write. Analytics owns the `analytics/raw/` prefix in it and its
		     objects are the ONLY copy once the table rows expire, so nothing
		     here expires. -->
		<S3BucketBlock bx:ref="archive" label="archive" deploy_versioned=false runtime_write=true object_versioning=true/>
		<!-- the block + its build artifact share one entity (the deploy pairs them) -->
		<LightsailBeetSiteBlock features="aws_sdk,ssh,geoip"/>
		<!-- the box is destroyed and recreated by every deploy, so it can own no
		     durable timer: the rollup runs as an invoke-only lambda the schedule
		     calls at 03:00 UTC, booting the `jobs` verb of this same document. -->
		<LambdaJobBlock bx:ref="rollup_fn" label="rollup" exec_route="jobs" features="aws_sdk,ssh,geoip,lambda"/>
		<ScheduledJobBlock label="rollup-daily" {InvokeTarget($rollup_fn)} schedule="cron(0 3 * * ? *)" path="rollup"/>
		<DeployRoutes/>
		<Route path="deploy" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<TofuApply layer="storage"/>
			<DirCopy src="assets" dest="site/assets" paths="extra/robot-eyes,databases/country.mmdb,wasm/beet-full.wasm"/>
			<DirSync bucket="app" local_dir="site" {SyncS3Bucket{delete:true}}/>
			<TofuApply/>
			<LightsailRelease/>
			<CloudflareZoneSetup/>
			<LightsailWatch timeout="300s"/>
			<CloudflarePurgeCache/>
		</Route>
		<Route path="sync" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<DirCopy src="assets" dest="site/assets" paths="extra/robot-eyes,databases/country.mmdb,wasm/beet-full.wasm"/>
			<DirSync bucket="app" local_dir="site" {SyncS3Bucket{delete:true}}/>
			<CloudflarePurgeCache/>
		</Route>
		<Route path="watch" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<LightsailWatch timeout="300s"/>
		</Route>
		<Route path="assets" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<DirCopy src="assets" dest="site/assets" paths="extra/robot-eyes,databases/country.mmdb,wasm/beet-full.wasm"/>
		</Route>
	</Stack>
	<Route path="shared" {RequireFeatures(["infra","extra"])}>
		<!-- the stage rides the `Stack` COMPONENT, so it is correct in every
		     binary, including the lean one where the verbs around it are inert -->
		<Stack stage="shared">
			<S3BucketBlock label="assets" deploy_versioned=false public_read=true force_destroy=false/>
			<DeployRoutes/>
			<Route path="push" {ExchangeSequence}>
				<DirCopy src="assets" dest="site/assets" paths="extra/robot-eyes,databases/country.mmdb,wasm/beet-full.wasm"/>
				<DirSync bucket="assets" local_dir="site/assets"/>
			</Route>
			<Route path="pull" {ExchangeSequence}>
				<DirSync bucket="assets" local_dir="site/assets" {SyncS3Bucket{direction:Pull, no_sign_request:true}}/>
			</Route>
		</Stack>
	</Route>
</Router>
</CliServer>
